// Test-suite type-check configuration.
//
// Extends the package tsconfig so the specs stay under the same `strict`
// family as the library, with two of the index-signature guards relaxed. Both are
// load-bearing for src/ (they catch dynamic option-object lookups) and
// actively hostile to tests, which traverse a parsed XML tree whose keys are
// only known at runtime:
//
//   noPropertyAccessFromIndexSignature — a parsed tree is accessed as
//     `result.root.tag['@_id']`. Without this rule, the leading `result.root`
//     dot access is allowed; with it, every traversal step would have to be
//     rewritten to bracket form purely to satisfy the checker.
//
//   noUncheckedIndexedAccess — a test asserting `result.item[0]['@_id']` is
//     checking that the parser produced the value. Making the type
//     `T | undefined` forces a null-check on every assertion, which would
//     obscure the thing under test behind type noise.
//
// The library keeps both rules (see ../tsconfig.json, which covers src/ and
// bench/). Only the test tree opts out, and only because its shape is
// genuinely dynamic.
//
// The `src/*.d.ts` files are pulled in explicitly: they carry the module
// augmentation that corrects `@nodable/entities`, which declares
// `EntityDecoder` as the module's *default* export while the runtime exports
// it by name. Without it the specs would see the published — and wrong —
// types. The augmentations that used to cover the `@nodable` builder
// packages are gone: the workspace replacements declare the real signatures,
// so the classes are assignable to the parser's structural contract
// without help.
{
  "extends": "../tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": { "noPropertyAccessFromIndexSignature": false, "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": false },
  "include": ["./**/*.ts", "../src/nodable-entities.d.ts"]
}
